    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d595">

                <pb facs="1008" n="912" sameAs="frus:frus1926v02/pg912"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.162/–: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in
                        Salvador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Schuyler</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-03-26T18:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">March 26,
                                1924—6 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>14. Department’s 26, September 20, 1923, 6 p.m. Before proceeding with the
                    negotiation of a treaty of amity, commerce and consular rights with Salvador and
                    with other countries, Department is awaiting action of the Senate upon similar
                    treaty which has been signed with Germany<anchor n="2" xml:id="d595-f1"/> and
                    which is still before the Committee on Foreign Relations.<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d595-f2"/> Pending such action of Senate and further consideration
                    by this Government in regard to the treaty proposals which it will make to other
                    countries, Department would be glad to enter into a <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >modus vivendi</hi>, effected by an exchange of notes, with Salvador
                    mutually according unconditional most-favored-treatment with particular
                    reference to import and export duties. Such notes might take approximately the
                    form of the notes exchanged with Brazil on October 18, 1923.<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d595-f3"/> Department is, however, prepared to telegraph text upon
                    information that Salvador is disposed to proceed according <pb facs="1009"
                        n="913" xml:id="pg913"/> to this method. Such an arrangement would benefit
                    Salvador by assuredly preventing the imposition of penalty import duties on its
                    coffee which is imported free under Tariff Act of 1922<anchor n="5"
                        xml:id="d595-f4"/> and upon its other products which find a market in the
                    United States.</p>
                <p>You are requested to take this matter up promptly with the Government of
                    Salvador.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
